title Two laps that stood on no ref
prompt Fleet baton -- petrichor seat, docs-geode and the prose product, Lindy-first crux-first.
rota lap 4557, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; read the threshold plus standfast and reds-first. Fire asks what must stop, and what it saw was this seat's own unsent record rather than anything I was about to build.
think Opened the roster cold and scoped. A stale --hot pass from a dead lap held the lock at pid 3057181; resolved it through /proc/<pid>/cwd to this tree, its launch_head a74c712240 already superseded so its verdict was fixed at tree_moved, and released it with one TERM to that pid alone.
think The pass cost 3,175 wall seconds over 214 guards -- 209 green, 4 red, 1 gated. Reds: tame_reach (a peer's lane, standing on the card), index_row_bound, stash_record, standing_equipment.
think fleet_round_open adopted the anointed order at 970c9fcb1d. index_row_bound had already been repaired by a peer in those 47 minutes -- rows_misordered 1 to 0 -- which is the fleet working, not me.
think stash_record read unlanded=2 and its list named the two: session-logs/date/20260910/20260910-074629 and 20260910-082330. Both are THIS seat's laps, written at 07:46 and 08:23, each GREEN by its own witness, each stashed by a round-open and never sent. A stash is neither tree nor ref, so nothing outside this checkout could see either.
think Before touching a byte I asked whether the work was still true. git diff <stash base> HEAD over every path in both stashes answered SAFE on all ten -- no peer had moved one since. That is what made restoration lawful rather than a rebuild, and it is the check I would want any lap to run first.
think git checkout <stash> -- <path> reads the TREE object, so the mode travels with the bytes; git show <ref>:<path> would have landed every file at 100644, which is the exec-bit law's own worked example.
obs Both witnesses re-prove on today's head rather than on their own: qa_report_card 159 behaviors, prose_register verdict=ok with door_over_ceiling=0. A recovered lap that only ever passed on its own base is a claim about a tree nobody has.
obs The first lap's repair is the one worth naming twice: qa_report_card scored docs-geode/README.md truth=100 and A+ 98 while its doorway figure was a quarter wrong, because the counted half asks whether cited PATHS resolve and a stale number is no path. truth_mode= now says which half answered.
obs I did not take the crux I had chosen. tutorial_output_scan reads one undeclared pair in docs-geode/tutorials/the-first-hour.md -- the run-it-twice block belongs to sh tools/f/fetch-toolchain.sh, which the page teaches nine lines above -- and a from: declaration checked against a command the page ALREADY runs would make it readable. Measured while reading: that command costs 71 ms and no network when the toolchain stands. Left as the next lap's door rather than opened beside a recovery.
obs REDS is at 173 bytes of headroom with every row OPEN, so nothing new could be booked. The stash class already has its row, %636, and this lap closed its instance rather than the question.
